    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "South Korea is currently experiencing two dramatic demographic shifts. The first is a demographic crisis due to a low birth rate and the second is a rapid increase in foreign population. As the Korean population decreases while migrant populations rise, the Korean government must reckon with the increasing diversity of its people. As Korea’s multiculturalism, or tamunhwa, entered the lexicon in the early 2000s, it became part of a public discourse and a state project. Rooted in histories of urbanization, colonialism, and ethnic nationalism, tamunhwa is closely tied to the family institution, where the multicultural category applies to families with one Korean parent, one non-Korean parent, and any of their children. This narrowly-defined multiculturalism leaves many immigrants on the margins of multicultural discourse, as well as without access to the many state-provided resources and centers designed for “multicultural families.” English teachers, international students, the Korean diaspora, and other long-term migrants are left outside of tamunhwa to varying effects. My research engages with the question: what are the effects of tamunhwa policy on immigrant populations in South Korea? Through interview-based qualitative data analysis, I explore the stories of diverse immigrant populations in South Korea in order to compare government and immigrant definitions of multiculturalism, analyze immigrant access to public resources, and identify room for policy change. Main findings include frustration at the narrow distribution of multicultural resources, some inaccuracies among immigrant definitions of tamunhwa, a sense of futility regarding integration, and difficulty settling down due to the need for frequent visa renewal. By engaging with the stories of immigrants within and outside of the definition of tamunhwa, and understanding their relationship to tamunhwa policy, I aim to identify areas where the South Korean government may better address the needs of its immigrant population.",
